# bootstrap/PROVENANCE — provenance for the bootstrap/teko.c currently committed to this tree.
#
# RESEED (hand-harvest, local on fix/retirement, forward-only): the §10 native-multithread futex
# SUBSTRATE — the atomic read-modify-write codegen intrinsics (M2 / plano §6.2, risk R5) — land in
# the checker + C backend, so the compiler's own emitted teko.c gains the new builtins' registration,
# dispatch, use-gate and helper emitter. What this harvest carries:
#   - **atomic RMW codegen intrinsics (compiler — checker + codegen)**: plano §6.2 / R5, the atomic
#     primitives the futex mutex and sequence condvar are built on. Without a real compare-and-swap
#     the mutex's 0->1 lock transition would race a plain `load_u64`+`store_u64`, so the sync leaf is
#     unsound until these exist. Four fixed-signature `i64`-word builtins, each lowered to a thin
#     `__atomic_*` helper (the raw-syscall-intrinsic precedent, one notch simpler — no per-arch `#if`
#     guard, the `__atomic_*` builtins are portable across x86_64/aarch64):
#       * `teko::sys::atomic_cas_u32(addr, expected, desired): i64` -> `tk_atomic_cas_u32`, a VALUE
#         compare-and-swap returning the observed word.
#       * `teko::sys::atomic_xchg_u32(addr, value): i64` -> `tk_atomic_xchg_u32`, unconditional swap.
#       * `teko::sys::atomic_add_u32(addr, delta): i64` -> `tk_atomic_add_u32`, fetch-and-add (the
#         condvar sequence `seq++` and the completed-worker counters).
#       * `teko::sys::atomic_load_u32(addr): i64` -> `tk_atomic_load_u32`, a seq-cst load.
#       * `src/checker/scope.tks` — `atomic_rmw_signature(nargs)` registers the four beside the
#         `syscall0..6`/`thread_clone` family as `Func { params = i64 x nargs; ret = i64 }`.
#       * `src/codegen/codegen.tks` — `emit_call` dispatch to the `tk_atomic_*` symbols; a single
#         `atomics` bit threaded through `SyscallUse` (set by `cg_mark_syscall_call`, scanned by the
#         existing `cg_scan_syscall_*` walk); `cg_emit_atomic_helpers` emits the four `static inline`
#         helpers ONLY when the program calls one of the intrinsics (a `static inline` unused helper
#         costs nothing and raises no `-Wunused-function`).
#   - **Inert in the compiler's own image**: the compiler never calls an atomic intrinsic in its own
#     source (the atomics live only in the §10 sync leaf, which is exercised by regression fixtures
#     built by gen1, NEVER by the compiler's corpus), so `SyscallUse.atomics` is always false for the
#     self-build and ZERO atomic helpers are emitted into teko.c (the only `tk_atomic_*` text in the
#     image is the string-literal helper body inside codegen's data) ⇒ a clean single-pass fixpoint,
#     exactly as the `syscallN` and `thread_clone` intrinsic keystones were.
#   - **Native leg honest-stops**: the atomic intrinsics are not in the native subset, so a call to
#     one falls through `native_builtin_symbol` (`src/lir/lower.tks`) to the terminal builtin stop —
#     the C leg is this pass's build+run target, the native lowering inherits them in Doc-2's terminal
#     phase (the identical posture `syscallN`/`thread_clone` take).
#   - Touch points: `src/checker/scope.tks`, `src/codegen/codegen.tks`.
#
# GATE: SELF-REPRODUCE — CLEAN FIXPOINT (tc1 == tc2 == tc3).
#   - The intrinsics are behavior-neutral for the self-build (dead code until a program calls an
#     atomic builtin), so gen0 (cc of the prior seed) and gen1 emit the same whole-tree bytes: the
#     C emitted by gen0 and by gen1 is byte-identical (sha256 below), a one-pass convergence.
#   - C-route chain: gen0 (cc, `-pthread`, from the prior bootstrap) built the tree -> gen1; gen1's
#     emitted C == gen0's emitted C; the C-route fixpoint gate returns PASSED (gen2 == gen3 byte for
#     byte, gen2.c == gen3.c). The four atomic helpers were validated standalone (a single-threaded
#     cas/xchg/add/load round-trip) and under real contention (an 8-thread futex-mutex counter and an
#     8-thread futex-condvar barrier, both exit 0). Self-reported peak ~5.7 GB (within the 6 GB cap).
